@startuml
' Startup and triggered-run flow for M1 (requirements/m1-run-timing-and-event-triggering.md, R20/R21).
' Nothing in this flow is a clock-synchronisation exchange: no node serves time to another,
' and no node compares its own clock against another node's timestamp.

title M1 startup and run flow — readiness by observation, alignment by configured offsets

actor "Operator" as OP
participant "CarSky\n(Nydus / Deployment Viewer)" as CS
participant "Bench — Scenario Player\n10.99.0.10" as BENCH
participant "V2X ECU\n10.99.0.11" as V2X
participant "ADA ECU core\n10.99.0.12" as ADA
participant "ADA detector\n(subprocess)" as DET
participant "IVI ECU (AAOS guest)\n10.99.0.13" as IVI

== Deployment — no ordering guarantee, no barrier ==

OP -> CS : New Deployment (blueprint)
CS -> IVI : start Skycraft guest (boots slowest)
CS -> V2X : start container
CS -> ADA : start container
CS -> BENCH : start container
note over CS
  The platform provides no dependsOn, no readiness probe,
  and no "deployment started" event delivered into a node.
  Start order is therefore undefined.
end note

== Readiness — observed, not handshaken (R5 acceptance) ==

V2X -> V2X : bind 47100, run R8 stub FSM
V2X --> CS : [EVT] ready
ADA -> DET : spawn detector
DET -> DET : load YOLO11n ONNX, open VIDEO_CLIP_PATH
DET --> CS : [EVT] ready
ADA -> ADA : bind 47200
ADA --> CS : [EVT] ready
IVI -> IVI : bind 47300 (R4 listener)
IVI --> CS : listener bound (logcat)
BENCH --> CS : [EVT] ready

OP -> CS : read per-node Running + restart 0,\nand the four ready lines
note right of OP
  This human check is R5's acceptance clause.
  It replaces a node-to-node readiness handshake.
end note

== Run start — restarting the bench is the GO ==

OP -> CS : Restart node (bench only)
CS -> BENCH : restart
BENCH -> BENCH : wait start_delay_s (scenario YAML)
BENCH -> BENCH : t = 0; monotonic deadline loop\nat cpm_rate_hz

== Paced streams — two independent sources, one scenario timeline ==

loop every 1/cpm_rate_hz (100 ms), deadline-scheduled on CLOCK_MONOTONIC
  BENCH -> BENCH : sample(t) -> CpmContent\n(referenceTime = ITS epoch ms)
  BENCH -> V2X : CPM (UPER, 58 B) over R6
  V2X -> V2X : stamp rxTime (CLOCK_REALTIME)\ndecode -> validate -> dedupe  [142-151 us]
  V2X -> ADA : R2 v2x_object (JSON, ~339 B)
  ADA -> ADA : store write\nmeasured = rxTime + timeOfMeasurement\nlastUpdated = ADA CLOCK_REALTIME
end

loop every DETECTOR_FRAME_STRIDE frames, paced to DETECTOR_CLIP_FPS
  DET -> DET : decode frame, infer, estimate distance to B
  DET -> ADA : R3 JSONL line (source = own_sensor)
  ADA -> ADA : store write, R13 admission
end

== Admission and warning — ordering constraint, not a clock constraint ==

ADA -> ADA : B reaches state = tracked\n(3 detections at 5 Hz)
... bench scenario lead-in: C closes 60 m -> 30 m over 12.0 s ...
ADA -> ADA : C crosses gate_enter, state = tracked\n(source = v2x_relayed)
ADA -> ADA : R14 risk transition
ADA -> IVI : R4 warning (ego, B, ghost C)
IVI -> IVI : render; auto-dismiss after\nWARNING_TIMEOUT_MS on the guest's own clock
note over IVI
  R4 carries no timestamp field.
  The guest never compares its clock to ADA's,
  so the AAOS clock domain is outside this design.
end note

== Verification — one clock, offline ==

OP -> ADA : download [EVT] JSONL
OP -> OP : ADA_ECU/tools/check_run_alignment.py\nK1-K5 (all stamped by ADA's clock alone)

@enduml
